i='TCS-3560';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 6, final paragraph: simultaneous size and degree for SOS, Positivstellensatz and Sherali–Adams, distinct from tightness of the degree bound.'
refs += [dict(id='degree',title='Size-Degree Trade-Offs for Sums-of-Squares and Positivstellensatz Proofs',authors='Albert Atserias; Tuomas Hakoniemi',year=2019,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CCC.2019.24',locator='Sections 2.1–2.4, twin variables, Boolean ideal and monomial-size conventions; Theorem 1, degree bound with PS product width w.'),dict(id='thesis',title='Size bounds for algebraic and semialgebraic proof systems',authors='Tuomas Hakoniemi',year=2022,url='https://upcommons.upc.edu/entities/publication/3541eaac-93a6-4b8c-b053-90ca851b5cff',locator='PhD thesis, defended 25 March 2022; Section 2.3.3, Sherali–Adams certificates and significant versus explicit monomials; Chapter 4, degree-reduction theorems.'),dict(id='bits',title='Monomial size vs. Bit-complexity in Sums-of-Squares and Polynomial Calculus',authors='Tuomas Hakoniemi',year=2021,url='https://doi.org/10.1109/LICS52264.2021.9470545',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2105.07525',locator='LICS 2021; abstract and main separation: degree-two, polynomial-monomial refutations can still require exponential binary coefficient size.'),dict(id='automatability',title='On the Degree Automatability of Sum-Of-Squares Proofs',authors='Alex Bortolotti; Monaldo Mastrolilli; Luis Felipe Vargas',year=2025,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2025.34',locator='Introduction and main criterion for classes admitting bounded-bit degree-d proofs; a proof-search/bit-complexity result, not the universal simultaneous monomial-size assertion.')]
issue='The source asks for conversion without blowing up size but does not state the admissible resulting size as a function of S, n and the input constraints, or distinguish existence from an effective conversion with a specified resource bound. For PS, the product-width parameter w must also be retained in the target. SOS, PS and SA are separately named systems, not interchangeable answers; the exact size convention and intended scope across them need confirmation before one universal quantified benchmark is certified.'
notes=['Recovered the named semialgebraic proof systems and simultaneous, rather than separate, resource target.','Defined Boolean twin-variable certificates, real coefficients, monomial counts and degree.','Restored the PS product-width dependence omitted by the source’s informal comparison.','Preserved unspecified allowable size increase and conversion requirement; checked 2021 bit separation, 2022 SA definitions and 2025 automatability results.']
save(i,dict(title='Simultaneous small size and low degree in semialgebraic refutations',formal='Can a small refutation in Sums-of-Squares (SOS), Positivstellensatz (PS), or Sherali–Adams (SA) be replaced by a refutation in the same system that simultaneously has the degree supplied by the size-to-degree theorem and controlled monomial size? The input is an unsatisfiable finite system of real polynomial inequalities and equalities on n Boolean variables, with maximum constraint degree k, and a refutation of monomial size S. For SOS the known degree target is O(k+√(n log S)); for PS of product width at most w it is O(kw+√(n log S)). The source asks whether this can be attained without a size explosion. It does not quantify the permitted size increase or whether algorithmic conversion is required, so the recovered question is not yet a single fully specified proposition.',definitions='Use commuting variables x₁,…,xₙ and their formal twins x̄₁,…,x̄ₙ over R. The Boolean ideal Iₙ is generated by xᵢ²−xᵢ, x̄ᵢ²−x̄ᵢ and xᵢ+x̄ᵢ−1 for every i. Thus the intended assignments have xᵢ∈{0,1} and x̄ᵢ=1−xᵢ. Input constraints are qⱼ≥0 and pᵢ=0, given as finite sums of real coefficients times monomials. An SOS refutation is an identity −1≡s₀+Σⱼsⱼqⱼ+Σᵢtᵢpᵢ modulo Iₙ, where each sⱼ is explicitly represented as Σₐrₐ,ⱼ² and tᵢ is arbitrary. It certifies that no intended assignment satisfies all constraints. In the cited 2019 convention its monomial size is the sum, with multiplicity across polynomials, of the numbers of nonzero monomials in all rₐ,ⱼ and tᵢ. Its degree is the maximum of deg(s₀), deg(sⱼ)+deg(qⱼ), and deg(tᵢ)+deg(pᵢ) over nonzero terms. Coefficient bit lengths and the multipliers of Boolean axioms are not counted. A PS refutation additionally allows terms sⱼ to multiply products Π_{j∈J}qⱼ; its product width is max|J|, and the corresponding degree includes Σ_{j∈J}deg(qⱼ). The same multiplier-based size convention excludes expansion of those input products. An SA certificate has the SOS identity’s shape but s₀ and sⱼ are polynomials with nonnegative coefficients, hence nonnegative on Boolean assignments; arbitrary squares of sums are unavailable. SA monomial accounting must specify whether it counts only these multipliers and tᵢ, or also input and Boolean-axiom polynomials, as distinguished in the 2022 thesis. The question concerns sparse monomial representations rather than arithmetic circuits or binary encodings of rational coefficients. The number of variables, constraint degrees and, for PS, product width are parameters; degree reduction alone gives no prescribed bound on the new certificate’s monomial size.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='After specifying the allowable size bound and whether the target is existential or algorithmic for the named system, establish both that size bound and the degree bound for the same refutation on every eligible input, or refute that universal statement. Separate proofs witnessing small size and low degree do not meet the target. Tightness of the degree-only theorem is a different question. The current passage leaves the acceptable size increase unspecified, and this review does not substitute a chosen polynomial bound.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The source’s theorem is about a weaker proof system',text='The paper proves that polynomial-calculus resolution cannot in general achieve its separate size and degree upper bounds simultaneously. Its conclusion asks about stronger semialgebraic systems; the PCR separation cannot simply be transferred to them.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The degree theorem has a PS parameter',text='Atserias and Hakoniemi’s theorem bounds degree while preserving the allowed product width. For SOS the width is one. Omitting w in the general PS formulation would silently change the known comparison.',citation='degree'),dict(title='Representation matters for a size question',text='Counting multipliers and counting expanded products can differ substantially for PS with large product width. Hakoniemi’s thesis also distinguishes significant monomials from all explicit monomials when presenting SA. An exact size-preservation claim must choose its convention.',citation='thesis'),dict(title='Coefficient size and proof search are separate',text='The 2021 result separates small monomial size and low degree from small binary coefficient representations. The 2025 work gives conditions supporting bounded-bit degree-based proof search. Neither is a universal simultaneous monomial-size-and-degree conversion theorem for this question.',citation='automatability')],why='Would determine whether sparsity and low algebraic degree can be enjoyed together in semialgebraic certificates, clarifying how proof size relates to the strength and cost of relaxation hierarchies.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2019',text='The cited theorem bounds SOS degree from monomial size and gives a product-width-sensitive PS analogue.',citation='degree'),dict(date='2020',text='The source asks whether these degree bounds can be attained together with small size in semialgebraic systems.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2021',text='A separation between monomial and bit size warns against reading the question as binary proof-length preservation.',citation='bits'),dict(date='2022',text='The thesis provides full SA definitions and degree-reduction proofs.',citation='thesis'),dict(date='2025',text='New sufficient conditions concern degree automatability and coefficient bounds, not the unresolved simultaneous size specification.',citation='automatability')],status='uncertain',status_note='Read the source conclusion and the cited degree theorem’s exact model and size conventions; checked the 2021 separation, 2022 thesis and 2025 automatability paper. No matching universal simultaneous-resource resolution was located through 12 September 2026. The acceptable size increase and precise multi-system scope remain unspecified.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(status='needs_specification',reviewed_on=DATE,notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue),review_note=issue,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A general compatibility question for sparse and low-degree certificates in central semialgebraic proof systems. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['A semialgebraic refutation certifies that Boolean polynomial constraints cannot all hold.','The source asks whether a proof can simultaneously have few monomials and the low degree guaranteed by existing size-to-degree theorems.','The systems named are Sums-of-Squares, Positivstellensatz and Sherali–Adams, with product width also relevant for Positivstellensatz.','The permissible increase in size and the exact conversion requirement are not specified in the source passage.','Later results on coefficient bit size and degree-based proof search address different resources and do not settle that missing target.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','degree','thesis','bits','automatability'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
